{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8bec3cbb404929b9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 1\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n11\t\n12\t**Strategy**: Operator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context\n13\t**Component scope**: rhods-operator (manager + cloudmanager binaries), ODH Operator team\n14\t\n15\t## Triage Decision (Step 0)\n16\t\n17\tBelow-threshold was evaluated and **does not apply**. The strategy is S-sized, affects a single\n18\tcomponent and a single code-owning team, and most scope scores High AI implementability — but the\n19\t**multiple distinct work streams** escape condition fires: the scope spans (a) mechanical code\n20\tconversion across two architecturally distinct binaries and (b) substantive documentation\n21\tauthoring — a new contribution guideline (new doc topic with required-field spec, camelCase\n22\trationale, three-problem diagnosis, before/after examples, DSCI key migration guide) that is\n23\tpublished for external operator adoption. That is substantive content authoring, not an incidental\n24\tdocumentation touch-up falling out of the code change, so the strategy proceeds to full\n25\tdecomposition. The genuine-unknowns escape was also checked: none of the strategy's open questions\n26\tchanges which epics exist or what they do (see Investigation Assessment below), so it does not\n27\tindependently trigger. Check 2 (docs-only) does not apply — rhods-operator requires code changes.\n28\t\n29\t## Staff Engineer Input (Step 1.5)\n30\t\n31\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. The strategy body\n32\talready incorporates its corrections, so no live divergence remained to override; the\n33\tdecomposition follows the SME-corrected architecture throughout:\n34\t\n35\t- Controller architecture is the SME-corrected **5 tiers** (DSC, 8 component controllers, module\n36\t  controller with 8 module handlers, DSCI, 5 service controllers) — not the 4-tier description the\n37\t  AI-generated draft had.\n38\t- Action files are the SME-confirmed **15 across 4 tiers** (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC +\n39\t  3 module); the discredited \"~16 component action files\" figure is not used.\n40\t- camelCase field naming (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) per SME input is adopted as the\n41\t  convention; the DSCI key names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) and the\n42\t  shared-reconciler/cloudmanager facts are taken from the SME input as authoritative.\n43\t\n44\t## Component Mapping (Step 2)\n45\t\n46\tThe strategy's only affected component, `rhods-operator` (manager + cloudmanager binaries), has\n47\tno verbatim entry in `.context/rhai-components.txt`. It is mapped to the closest canonical name\n48\t**OpenShift AI Productization** for all three epics (the RHOAI platform operator that assembles\n49\tand productizes OpenShift AI). This is a non-obvious mapping, logged here per the component-name\n50\tconstraint; `submit.py` would silently strip the literal string `rhods-operator`.\n51\t\n52\tThe component is **active** (code changes) and **in the platform**: an architecture context file\n53\texists (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`), so no\n54\tonboarding chain (repo/license/konflux) is generated. The Prerequisites table's \"Pending human\n55\treview\" entries are template placeholders for process gates, not signals of missing\n56\tinfrastructure.\n57\t\n58\t## Epic List\n59\t\n60\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n61\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n62\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Manager binary reconciliation error log conversion to structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n63\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Cloudmanager binary reconciliation error log conversion to structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n64\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Structured log field convention guideline and external operator publication | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n65\t\n66\t## Dependency DAG\n67\t\n68\t```mermaid\n69\tgraph TD\n70\t    E001[\"E001 — Manager binary log conversion (P0)\"]\n71\t    E002[\"E002 — Cloudmanager binary log conversion (P0)\"]\n72\t    E003[\"E003 — Convention guideline + publication (P1)\"]\n73\t```\n74\t\n75\tAll three epics are roots; the DAG has no edges. Critical path length: 1.\n76\t\n77\t## DAG Justification\n78\t\n79\t| Pair | Rule | Rationale |\n80\t|------|------|-----------|\n81\t| E001 ∥ E002 | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) | Manager and cloudmanager are separate binaries with disjoint controller sets; parallel work carries only merge-coordination risk, not a dependency. Neither produces an artifact the other builds against — both consume the field convention, which is fully specified by the strategy/SME input, not produced by an epic. |\n82\t| E001 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 (no shared build-against artifact) | The contribution guideline documents a convention already specified in the strategy (Staff Engineer input fixed the fields and camelCase naming); it does not describe what the code epics discover. The strategy's own risk mitigation calls for publishing the convention early, so serializing it after the code would contradict the strategy. |\n83\t| E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 (no shared build-against artifact) | Same rationale as above; the guideline's cloudmanager-relevant content is fixed by the strategy. |\n84\t\n85\t**Why E003 is a general Implementation, not `docs-authoring`**: the deliverable is a repo-level\n86\tcontribution guideline plus an external publication spec for the COO team — it is not a Red Hat\n87\tdocs content process deliverable, so the `docs-authoring` routing label (and with it the\n88\tRule 11 docs-last edge set) does not apply. The guideline is nonetheless a separate epic because\n89\tsubstantive content authoring is a distinct work-product type from the code conversion\n90\t(work-product type boundary, Step 5 rule 2).\n91\t\n92\t**CI lint rule — divided per surface, not extracted**: the strategy's enforcement deliverable (a\n93\tCI lint rule flagging `log.Error` calls that embed identity in message text or miss the required\n94\tstructured fields) spans the same surfaces the conversion epics convert. Per the shared-surface\n95\tenforcement rule it is NOT a standalone work-type epic: E001 authors and verifies the lint-rule\n96\tcoverage for the manager binary's converted call sites, and E002 authors and verifies the coverage\n97\tfor the cloudmanager binary's call sites, each as Scope plus a verification AC.\n98\t\n99\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n100\t\n101\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n102\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n103\t| HLR-1 — name/namespace structured KV conversion of message-embedded identity | P0 | E001, E002 | `rhods-operator` spans both binaries; each epic converts its own binary's call sites. |\n104\t| HLR-2 — DSCI migration from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase keys | P0 | E001 | DSCI controller lives in the manager binary. |\n105\t| HLR-3 — service controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | All 5 service controllers are manager-binary controllers. |\n106\t| HLR-4 — all 15 action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | All 15 action files (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) are manager-binary files. |\n107\t| HLR-5 — cloudmanager controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | Cloudmanager binary's 3 controllers. |\n108\t| HLR-6 — module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | Module controller + 8 module handlers are manager-binary scope. |\n109\t| HLR-7 — DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P1 | E001 (bundled) — `resourceKind` is added to the very same `log.Error` call sites in the same DSC/component controller files being converted for the P0 HLRs; a split would re-edit and re-audit the same files twice, so it is not separately schedulable. | Bundling recorded at the row per the Step 4 contract. |\n110\t| HLR-8 — document convention as contribution guideline and publish for external adoption | P1 | E003 | Substantive doc authoring + external publication; separate work stream. |\n111\t\n112\tAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n113\t\n114\t## Investigation Assessment (Step 3)\n115\t\n116\tAll unknowns were collected from the open questions table, risks, assumptions, and pending\n117\treviews, then evaluated against \"does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they\n118\tdo?\" None qualifies — no Investigation epics:\n119\t\n120\t- **Extended fields beyond name/namespace/kind** (PM / Observability): the required field set is\n121\t  fixed by the HLRs and SME input; the answer changes only guideline content, not epic\n122\t  structure. Disposition recorded in E003 scope/AC (required fields documented; extended fields\n123\t  given an explicit included-or-deferred disposition).\n124\t- **Which alerts rely on message-string patterns** (SRE / Support): identification is work\n125\t  embedded in the log-consumer regression verification each conversion epic already performs;\n126\t  no epic's existence or approach changes. Captured as an AC plus a coordination risk on both\n127\t  conversion epics (E001 for the manager binary's Loki consumer, E002 for the cloudmanager\n128\t  binary's CloudWatch consumer).\n129\t- **Cloudmanager same release or subsequent** (PM): a scheduling decision only — the work is\n130\t  identical either way. The epic boundary already isolates cloudmanager so it can follow a\n131\t  later release train without restructuring; E002 stays P0 because HLR-5 is P0.\n132\t- **Actual percentage of embedded-identity calls** (audit): the audit is part of each conversion\n133\t  epic's own work (grep/static analysis + tracked checklist); conversion happens for 100% of\n134\t  identified sites regardless of the percentage. Captured as an AC on E001 and E002, not an\n135\t  Investigation.\n136\t- The Prerequisites \"pending human review\" entries are template process gates, not conditional\n137\t  ADRs; nothing in them changes the epic structure.\n138\t\n139\t## Health Warnings\n140\t\n141\t1. **E001 size over the ~2-week guideline, kept whole**: E001 is ~1.25–1.5 sprints by the\n142\t   strategy's effort arithmetic (the manager-binary share of the ~0.5-sprint audit plus the\n143\t   ~0.75–1-sprint manager conversion and its lint-rule share). Kept whole because the conversion\n144\t   is one mechanical pass over the same `log.Error` call sites and splitting it by controller\n145\t   tier would re-edit the same files and audit them twice. Re-split trigger: the call-site audit\n146\t   exceeds the strategy's 75% embedded-identity estimate (or manager conversion effort exceeds\n147\t   ~1.5 sprints), pushing the epic past 2 sprints — at that point split by controller tier\n148\t   (component/module vs. service/DSCI) with the audit checklist divided accordingly.\n149\t2. **P1 bundled into P0 (HLR-7)**: `resourceKind` for DSC/component controllers rides in E001\n150\t   with the bundling rationale recorded in the traceability row above; it is not a deferrable\n151\t   feature because it shares the exact call sites and files with the P0 conversion.\n152\t3. **Cross-team coordination risk (not a DAG edge)**: identifying and updating text-based alert\n153\t   rules requires SRE/Support involvement (AC on both conversion epics — E001 for the manager\n154\t   binary's Loki-side rules, E002 for the cloudmanager binary's CloudWatch-side rules); their\n155\t   response time is externally controlled and may extend the epics' calendar time without\n156\t   indicating engineering incomplete.\n157\t4. **Log consumer regression risk**: message strings change (identity removed after moving to\n158\t   structured fields); the before/after ingestion verification is an AC on both conversion epics,\n159\t   and the DSCI key-name migration must land in a single release to avoid long-term query\n160\t   inconsistency (release-notes documentation is folded into E003's migration guide).\n161\t5. **Proposed acceptance criteria**: the strategy's ACs are marked \"requires PM/Engineering\n162\t   validation\"; the epic ACs below are derived from them as written. If PM validation changes a\n163\t   \"measured by\" clause, the affected AC — not the epic structure — changes.\n164\t6. **Open question on extended fields** is discharged at the documentation level (E003 records\n165\t   the disposition) rather than blocked on a PM decision; if PM later adds fields, that is an\n166\t   increment to the same surfaces, not new epic structure.\n167\t\n168\t## Tiered Delivery\n169\t\n170\tNot applicable. No external dependency gates any RHOAI-side deliverable: the controller-runtime\n171\tlogr capability exists, and COO team (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) adoption of the published\n172\tconvention is voluntary per platform policy overlay 0008 — it is out of RHOAI's control, so no\n173\tCOO epic exists and no tiered delivery is modeled. E003 handles the RHOAI-side obligation:\n174\tpublish the convention and file tracking issues in the COO projects.\n175\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-857ead3cbe10d32a","agent_id":"a60ddb101ee3fd566","session_id":"d69bb09f-72b1-4df0-bf0b-d16ae9dbfdec","uuid":"6a0d7ebb-6fec-4d73-80d1-02803d9cf9ec","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:40:51.686Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8bec3cbb404929b9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8010"}
